 ## Overview
-Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction refers to methods that infer the spatial attributes (position, covariance, and opacity) of 3D Gaussian primitives directly from input data, rather than optimizing them via iterative gradient descent per scene. It solves the latency bottleneck of standard 3D Gaussian Splatting (3DGS) [Parent](2313-gaussian-splatting-initialization-and-parameterization.md) by enabling feed-forward reconstruction. Unlike sibling initialization methods that rely on static heuristics or single-frame depth [6129-random-large-variance-initialization.md], these methods leverage learned neural volumes or multi-view consistency to predict geometry that is globally consistent and robust to noise.
+Gaussian Geometry Parameters Prediction is a method for initializing and refining the geometric attributes (position, opacity, and covariance) of 3D Gaussian primitives in a feed-forward or iterative manner, typically leveraging a global volumetric representation rather than per-pixel local volumes [S2]. It solves the problem of redundant, inconsistent, and noisy Gaussian initialization found in standard pixel-aligned methods, which often lead to ghosting artifacts and poor global consistency [S2]. Unlike siblings that rely on heuristic densification or static point clouds, this method uses a learned neural volume to predict geometry offsets and attributes, enabling efficient reconstruction of large-scale scenes [S2].
 
 ## How it works
-The primary implementation of this method is **EVolSplat** [S2], which predicts a global scene representation using a 3D convolutional network.
